  "verdict": "REFUTE the naive SU(2)/WRT(S3/2I) route as a reproduction of the full W_lambda family. COVERAGE: 7 blocks across n=3,4,5 have >=3 rows and lie OUTSIDE the GL_2 (spinor C^2) Schur-Weyl image -- the SU(2) side assigns them no weight (incl. the electron (3,1,1) at n=5). VALUE: of 8 <=2-row blocks, only 3 have d_q(lambda) == [2j+1]_q exactly ([{'n': 3, 'lambda': [2, 1]}, {'n': 4, 'lambda': [3, 1]}, {'n': 5, 'lambda': [4, 1]}]); the rest differ (e.g. muon (3,2): d_q=[5]_q vs [2]_q). Consistent with #2608's Schur-Weyl duality (WRT SU(2)-side != W_lambda S_n-side, coincide only at (4,1)-type) and #2406's 'no finite colour-SU(N) arena'. The geometric W_lambda index MUST live on the full-Hecke (infinite-dim) side, not the SU(2)/WRT(S3/2I) modular data. NORMALIZATION: per-block ratios within fixed n are identical in the [n]_q! and D^2 conventions (global factor cancels); only the total Z(n) -- the defect 1-Z(n) -- is normalization-sensitive, so the defect (not the absolute per-block value) is the fixed falsifiable content.",
